  "summary": "Regional variant of Mannerist architecture in Lombardy characterized by spatial complexity, sculptural forms, and dramatic contrasts between light and shadow.",
  "description": "Lombard Mannerism developed as a distinctive response to both High Renaissance and emerging Baroque influences, creating buildings with dynamic compositions, broken pediments, and sculptural details that emphasized three-dimensional form over classical harmony. It reflected Milan's position as a center of artistic innovation under Spanish rule.",
  "culturalContext": "Lombard Mannerism — Galeazzo Alessi, Pellegrino Tibaldi, Martino Bassi — ran Milan's Counter-Reformation building into spatial experiments: Alessi's Marino and Anguissola palaces, Tibaldi's Piacenza Farnese and Milan Cathedral refits, perspective ambiguity, rusticated order-stacking and court-stage lobbies. The school carried Tuscan Mannerist grammar northward into church-and-court commissions and seeded the transition to Milan's Baroque; its cortili remain the city's hidden-room architecture.",
